+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+//
+// The integrated GBA's screen and buttons in a browser: http://<console>:6771/
+//
+// The GBA runs in-process (HW::GBA::Core); this only watches it. Its finished frames go to a
+// one-slot mailbox (the newest frame wins), a stream thread per browser sends them, and the
+// page's buttons come back as a key mask that SI_DeviceGBAEmu ORs into the GBA's keys. Nothing
+// here can make the emulation wait: the GBA thread only takes a mutex for a memcpy-sized copy,
+// every socket is non-blocking, and a browser that stops reading is dropped after two seconds.
+// Its messages are Dolphin's (SERIALINTERFACE: errors and warnings always, the rest at Info and
+// Debug). Plain POSIX sockets, not SFML: SFML's listener cannot set SO_REUSEADDR, and without it the
+// port stays unusable for a minute after the content closes.
+//
+// Protocol: GET /  the page;  GET /frame?min=<seq>&p=<port 1-4>  the first frame numbered seq or
+// later (or ?after=<seq>: any frame other than seq), so the page can keep two requests in flight
+// for the next two frames: 200 with a fixed length body and X-Seq, or 204 after a second of
+// nothing new, with the current X-Seq; the page asks when it is ready, so it sets the pace and
+// nothing can back up;
+// add &z=1 for 'GBA2': width, height, a u32 length, then the pixels compressed (CompressFrame);
+// A frontend that maps the GBA's buttons and shows its screen itself (Porpoise) calls two plain C
+// functions it looks up in the core: dolphin_gba_set_keys, dolphin_gba_get_frame,
+// dolphin_gba_set_web_server and dolphin_gba_reset (below).
+// GET /stream?p=&div=  the same frames as one never-ending response;  a frame is 'G' 'B' 'A' '1',
+// width (u16 LE), height (u16 LE), then width*height u16 pixels (bit 15 unused, bits 14-10 blue,
+// 9-5 green, 4-0 red: the GBA's own 15-bit colour, so nothing is lost);  GET /k?m=<mask>&p=<port>
+// the held buttons (mGBA's key bits), 204 on a kept-alive connection;  GET /state  the active
+// ports.
